  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Complicated forecast ahead with a chance of precipitation.\n\nClear skies and calm to light winds continues today through\nFriday as a surface high pressure remains over the Mid- \nAtlantic. The center of this high looks to weaken in the coming \ndays with a piece breaking away and migrating more to the \nnortheast and wedging into the mountains. The warming trend will\nreach its peak by Friday with highs in the 60s and potentially \nreaching 70 in the Piedmont. \n\nAfter that, things get complicated. Models have agreement in an \nupper level trough to our west and a coastal low off the Carolinas \nforming this weekend. After initiation, the models have severe \ndisagreements on the strength, speed, and direction of these \nsystems, each with their own scenarios of potential impacts to \nour area in regards to winds, rain, and cloud cover. Focusing on\nthe coastal low first, the system does look to travel northward\nand at least as far west as along the coastline, potentially \nproviding easterly winds, increased cloud cover, and \nprecipitation for the Piedmont of NC and VA. The diverse range \nof paths means there is little certainty on the amount of \nrainfall at this moment so this will be updated in future \nforecasts. Switching to the upper level low to the west, this \nwave will play a factor in the trajectory of the coastal low but\nmodels also disagree on the behavior of this trough. Part of \nthe challenge is the trough to our west may be carrying a \nshortwave ahead of it and place it in between the main trough \nand the coastal low. In addition, if the short wave pushes \nthrough the Mid-Atlantic, then there may be a chance of upslope\nprecipitation in the mountains this weekend as well. The \ninteraction between the coastal low and the Midwestern trough \nand its racing shortwave overall make for a headache of a \nforecast. Overall message is there may be a change to the clear\nskies for this weekend into early next week with a chance for\nsome much needed rain for the Piedmont.",
